 104: def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:
 105:     """
 106:     Extend each path object to its matched marker and recolor an equal-length tail from the path's opposite endpoint.
 107: 
 108:     Input structure
 109:     - The background is the dominant color.
 110:     - Non-background pixels form the same number of isolated marker pixels and 4-connected monochrome path objects.
 111:     - Each path object is a one-pixel-wide, unbranched orthogonal path with exactly two endpoints.
 112:     - Every marker has a clear row or column sightline to at least one path endpoint, and the global marker-to-path assignment is unique.
 113: 
 114:     Rule
 115:     - Match markers to path objects by the unique one-to-one assignment where each marker sees its object's endpoint along a straight background gap.
 116:     - Fill that gap with the matched path object's color, leaving the marker pixel itself unchanged.
 117:     - From the opposite endpoint of the same path, recolor as many existing path cells with the marker color as there were gap cells.
 118: 
 119:     Key insight
 120:     - The marker does not simply color the nearby endpoint; its distance from the path measures how much of the far end of that path is converted.
 121:     """
